what does it mean for a matrix to be  positive we know since elementary school  what it means for a real number to be  positive for example 2 is a positive  number minus 3 is not but what about  matrices can we find a sensible useful  definition of what it means for a matrix  to be positive the goal of this video is  to show that the answer is yes  and make no mistake this new definition  that we will see is not simply a  mathematical curiosity in fact this new  definition has enabled a whole new field  of mathematics called semi-definite  programming with some unexpected and  frankly mind-boggling applications to  science and engineering  here are by order of difficulty three  math questions that we will attack using  this new notion the first question is  from geometry consider three unit  vectors x y and z and assume that the  inner products between each pair of  vectors is the same and let's call the  common value of this inner product alpha  in other words assume that if you  project x on y y on z or z on x the sine  length of the projection is the same  alpha another question is what is the  smallest possible value for alpha we  already know that alpha is between -1  and 1 and the following configuration  where all the vectors are orthogonal to  each other shows that alpha can take the  value 0.  so the minimum value of alpha is  somewhere between -1 and 0 but what is  it exactly  the second question is from dynamical  system theory given a square matrix a  can you tell whether the linear  dynamical system given by xk plus 1  equals a times xk is asymptotically  stable  that is can you prove or disprove that  for any given initial condition  if you keep multiplying by a you always  convert to zero  the third and last question i wanted to  show you is about finding good solutions  to np-hard problems  for example in the famous max cut  problem you are given a graph and you  want to cut the notes in two sets in  such a way that the value of this cut or  in other words the number of edges that  the cut goes through is as high as  possible  this is an imperative problem which  means that it is highly unlikely that an  efficient algorithm for finding the best  possible cut exists  can you think of an algorithm that finds  a good enough cut  or in other words can you find a cut  whose value is at least let's say 0.8 of  the value of the maximal cut i totally  can see that this is somewhat of a  question at this point but trust me the  solution technique is so beautiful that  it was impossible for me not to include  this question in this video  amazingly all of these questions were  used to find in a particular positive  matrix that satisfy some conditions and  if you're curious about this new  definition of matrix positivity and  you're wondering how it is even possible  to use this notion to tackle so many  different problems that are not even  about matrices in the first place then  this is the perfect video for you the  only prerequisite you will need is some  familiarity with the basics of linear  algebra if you know what a vector is  what a matrix is and what it means to  multiply matrices and vectors together  then you're good to go  [Music]  so how can we generalize positivity for  matrices as usual in mathematics to find  the right generalization of some concept  like positivity you need to look at it  from the right perspective sure we could  define positivity element wise we could  define a matrix to be positive if all of  its entries are positive but this  definition is not really satisfying  because it does not use the matrix  structure in any meaningful way what i  mean by that is that we could rearrange  all the terms in a column vector a row  vector or even in a box the definition  would still go through there is a much  more useful definition of positivity for  matrices and more specifically for  symmetric matrices that takes into  account the matrix structure  why only symmetric matrices you might  ask  well it turns out that with the  symmetric assumption the math just  becomes particularly nice and most of  the cases of interest in practice  involve symmetric matrices anyway so  what makes a matrix a matrix is not the  fact that it is a table of values but  rather it is the fact that it is a  linear operator that acts on vectors it  takes as input a vector x and spits out  another vector y equals a times x  to gain some intuition consider the  special case where the matrix a is one  by one that is a number  if a is positive for example equal to  two you can think of multiplication by a  as a stretch in operation  on the other hand if a is negative for  example -2 then you can think of  multiplication by a as a reflection  about the origin and then stretching  so positive numbers when you think of  them as operators are really the ones  that keep their output on the same side  as their input with this in mind we  define a matrix a to be positive if for  any non-zero vector x the vector y that  a outputs stays on the same side as x  in other words the inner product between  x and a times x should be positive and  to make it clear that we're talking  about positivity for matrices and not  numbers we will call such a matrix  positive definite if the inner product  between x and ax is only in a negative  we call the matrix a in that case  positive  semi-definite before we move any further  let's look at some special cases to get  a feel for this new definition  the identity matrix for example is  positive definite  this is because if you multiply the  identity from the left and the right by  some non-zero vector x you get x  transpose x which is the sum of the  entries of x squared which is positive  you can also directly argue that the  identity is positive definite because it  does not change its input so obviously  the input and the output fall on the  same side now let's take a look at a  diagonal matrix that is not necessarily  the identity to be able to tell whether  it's positive definite let's multiply it  on the left and the right by some  non-zero vector x  in this case we get the sum of the  diagonal elements times the square of  the entries of x if all the diagonal  elements are positive then its diagonal  matrix is positive definite if one or  more entries is zero then it is only  positive semi-definite  and if at least one of the diagonal  entries is negative then this matrix is  not even positive semi-definite and here  you might wonder if that  characterization is true for  non-diagonal symmetric matrices as well  to know if a symmetric matrix is  positive semi-definite can we just check  if the diagonal entries are negative  this is actually false for example this  symmetric matrix  has positive diagonal entries but you  can easily check that when you multiply  by the vector 1 1 from the left and the  right which corresponds to and this is a  good trick to know summing up all the  entries of the matrix  negative 2. so this matrix is not  positive semi-definite so it turns out  that the right numbers to look at are  not the diagonal elements but rather the  eigenvalues of the matrix  recall that eigenvalues are numbers  lambda that satisfy a times u equals  lambda u for some non-zero vector u  which is known as the eigenvector  you see for diagonal matrices the  diagonal elements are exactly the  eigenvalues but that stops being true  for non-diagonal matrices let's see  what's being positive semi-definite  means for eigenvectors and eigenvalues  by definition if you apply h1  eigenvector u it gets multiplied by an  eigenvalue lambda if lambda is a  negative you simply get scaled  and if lambda is negative u gets  reflected and then scaled  so the only way for matrices to be  positive some by definite is if all of  its eigenvalues are negative  the converse although we won't have time  to prove is also true if all the  eigenvalues are negative then the matrix  is positive so my definite  this new characterization is important  because it gives us one algorithmic way  of checking a matrix positivity it  reduces the task of checking whether a  matrix is positive semi-definite to  checking whether a bunch of numbers the  eigenvalues aren't a negative for  example the eigenvalues of this matrix  that we have seen before r5 and -1 which  is negative so this matrix is not  positive semi-definite  to recap what we have seen up to now we  have defined positive semi-definite  matrices to be the ones that satisfy x a  x and negative for any non-zero vector x  and we showed that this is equivalent to  the negativity of the eigenvalues  and note that this generalizes as you  would expect to positive definite  matrices  it's now time to put this knowledge to  good use so to end this video let's  reinforce our geometrical understanding  of positive semi-definite matrices by  looking at sets that are described by  matrix inequalities  and let's start with scalar inequalities  for example let's look at triplets x y z  that satisfy x non-negative y negative  zenith negative and x plus y plus z  smaller or equal than one these  inequalities describe a pyramid like set  you know you are dealing with scalar  inequalities because the faces of this  set are flat  moving on what if we look now at  inequalities that involve matrices of  size strictly bigger than one for  example for what values of x this matrix  is positive semi-definite  it's not very hard to check that the  eigenvalues of this matrix are 1 plus x  and 1 minus x so for it to be positive  sum indefinite we need x to be at least  -1 and at most one so a single matrix  inequality can be rewritten as few  scalar inequalities is this always true  is matrix inequality simply a compressed  version of multiple scalar inequalities  as we will see the next example that is  not true matrix positivity is a much  more powerful concept than just a mere  collection of scalar inequalities let's  look at the set of triplets x y z for  which this matrix is positive  semi-definite writing out the  eigenvalues in terms of x y and z is  possible but very cumbersome but for any  particular values of x y and z we can  easily check if the eigenvalues of the  corresponding matrix are non-negative or  not  for example for the triplet zero zero  zero this matrix has a single eigenvalue  equal to one so that triplet is inside  our set on the other hand for the  triplet two two two the eigenvalues are  the numbers five and minus one so this  triplet is outside our set and by  looking at all possible triplets we can  see what kind of set a matrix inequality  like this one can describe  and that's where things get interesting  triplets xyz that satisfy this matrix  inequality or geometrically the set  composed of all the green dots is called  an elliptop and it has some fascinating  properties  note in particular that its faces are  not flat so it cannot be expressed by  the usual scalar inequalities so  positivity for matrices is a true  generalization of positivity for scalars  and we will end things here for now but  stay tuned for the next video because  that's where the magic will happen  we will see how to use this set to  answer the first geometry question that  i presented in this video as usual if  you liked the video please like and  subscribe and see you next time

